==========================================================
WHY DOES `--` APPEAR TWICE?
==========================================================

npm create vite@latest  my-vehicle-app  --  --template react
└──────┬──────────┘    └──────┬──────┘  │   └──────┬───────┘
   npm command          folder name     │      option passed
                                        │      to Vite
                                  tells npm: "what comes
                                  after is NOT for me,
                                  pass it to the tool below"

- First `--`: Tells npm, "Pass the arguments that follow
  to the `create-vite` tool"

- `--template react`: The actual option sent to that tool

- Note: Some npm versions work without `--`, but it is
  always safer to include it.


==========================================================
WHAT DOES THIS COMMAND DO?
==========================================================

- Creates a folder named `my-vehicle-app`

- Sets up a React + Vite project structure inside it:

my-vehicle-app/
├── src/
│   ├── App.jsx
│   ├── main.jsx
│   └── index.css
├── public/
├── index.html
├── package.json
└── vite.config.js


==========================================================
COMMANDS TO RUN AFTERWARD
==========================================================

cd my-vehicle-app      # Move into the folder
npm install            # Install dependencies
npm run dev            # Start the development server


==========================================================
6. package.json — PROJECT ID CARD
==========================================================

This is the project's identity + dependencies list.

Type            | Description
----------------|------------------------------------------
dependencies    | Needed in production (react, react-dom)
devDependencies | Needed only in development (vite, eslint)


{
  "name": "my-vehicle-app",
  "private": true,
  "version": "0.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",              // npm run dev
    "build": "vite build",      // npm run build
    "preview": "vite preview",  // npm run preview
    "lint": "eslint ."
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "react": "^19.0.0",
    "react-dom": "^19.0.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@vitejs/plugin-react": "^4.3.4",
    "vite": "^6.0.0"
  }
}


==========================================================
`npm run dev` — WHAT DOES NPM DO?
==========================================================

It looks up the `scripts` section in package.json:

"scripts": {
  "dev": "vite"        <-- It searches for this
}

It finds that the value of `dev` is `vite`, and runs
the `vite` command.

In reality:
  npm run dev  =  npx vite  =  runs node_modules/.bin/vite


==========================================================
STEP 2: WHAT DOES VITE DO?
==========================================================

Once Vite starts running, it:

1. Reads vite.config.js
2. Loads the React plugin
3. Starts a Dev Server
4. Asks the OS for a port (default: 5173)
5. Begins listening on that port


==========================================================
STEP 3: WHERE DOES PORT 5173 COME FROM?
==========================================================

5173 is Vite's default port — it is not a random number!

"5173" looks like "SITE" in leetspeak
5 = S
1 = I
7 = T
3 = E

Yes! The creators of Vite wrote "SITE" in leetspeak
and used it as the port number.


==========================================================
localhost:5173 — WHAT DOES IT MEAN?
==========================================================

http://localhost:5173
└─┬─┘  └───┬───┘ └┬─┘
protocol  host   port

Part       | Meaning
-----------|-----------------------------------------------
http://    | Protocol (not HTTPS, plain HTTP for local)
localhost  | Your own computer (127.0.0.1) — refers to
           | this machine itself
5173       | Which port on that machine to look at


WHAT DOES `localhost` MEAN?

localhost is a special hostname — it refers to your
own computer.

localhost  =  127.0.0.1  =  "me" (this computer)


